/* TechTrail Academy curriculum. Concepts are designed to stay useful; model specifications can change. */
const COURSES = [
 {id:'phones',name:'Phone Mastery',short:'Phones',emoji:'📱',color:'#7263e8',banner:'banner-phone',level:'Beginner → Advanced',tagline:'Understand every number on the box.',description:'From the parts inside your phone to camera science, networks, privacy, troubleshooting and confident buying.',duration:'5–7 hours',lessons:[
 {title:'Meet your smartphone',level:'Beginner',mins:12,desc:'What a phone is, what its main parts do, and how they work together.',intro:'A smartphone is a tiny general-purpose computer that fits in your pocket. It takes input (your touch, voice, camera), processes it, stores information, and produces output (the screen, sound and vibration). Most things you do are a team effort between several parts—not one magic chip.',analogy:'Imagine a tiny school: the processor is the principal making quick decisions, RAM is the desk where today’s books are open, storage is the library cupboard, and the screen is the noticeboard. If the desk is crowded, you can still own a huge library and feel slow!',sections:[{title:'The main parts',body:'The system-on-a-chip (SoC) includes processing components. RAM holds information currently needed. Storage keeps your apps, photos and files after the phone powers off. The battery supplies energy, while power-management circuits decide how that energy is used.',bullets:['Touchscreen: both a display and an input surface on most phones.','Sensors: detect things such as motion, light, orientation and proximity.','Modem and antennas: connect to cellular networks, Wi‑Fi, Bluetooth and GPS-related signals.','Operating system: coordinates apps, hardware, permissions and updates.']},{title:'Follow a simple action',body:'When you tap a photo, the touchscreen reports your finger position. The operating system sends the event to the app. The processor runs the app’s instructions, RAM holds working data, and the display controller updates the pixels. The battery powers every step.'}],diagram:[['👆','Touch','Your input'],['🧠','Processor','Runs instructions'],['🗂️','RAM','Working space'],['🖼️','Display','Shows result']],example:{title:'Real-life example',body:'Opening a camera app uses the processor, RAM, image sensor, storage (when you save a photo), screen and battery at the same time.'},myth:'“The phone with the most expensive chip must be best for everyone.” A powerful chip can help with demanding games and editing, but software support, screen, battery, camera and price also matter.',takeaways:['A phone is a system of parts working together.','RAM is temporary working space; storage keeps files.','The operating system coordinates apps and hardware.'],quiz:[{q:'Which part normally keeps your photos when the phone is switched off?',options:['RAM','Internal storage','Screen refresh rate','Speaker'],answer:1,why:'Internal storage retains data without power; RAM is temporary working memory.'},{q:'You tap an icon and the app opens. What best describes this?',options:['Only the battery is working','The screen, software, processor and memory cooperate','The camera stores the tap','The charger runs the app'],answer:1,why:'Ordinary actions use several connected hardware and software components.'},{q:'Which is the best mental model for a smartphone?',options:['One single magic chip','A collection of parts coordinated by software','A screen with a battery only','A camera that can call people'],answer:1,why:'Smartphones combine compute, memory, storage, connectivity, sensors, power and software.'}]},
 {title:'Decode the spec sheet',level:'Beginner',mins:14,desc:'Learn what RAM, GB, Hz, nits, mAh and IP ratings actually mean.',intro:'A spec sheet is like a food label for technology: useful, but only when you know what the numbers measure. Units matter. GB is storage capacity; Hz is cycles per second; nits measure brightness; mAh describes electric charge capacity, not a direct promise of battery life.',analogy:'A 1,000-litre water tank is not automatically better than a smaller tank if the pump leaks. In the same way, a large battery number does not guarantee longer runtime if the phone uses more energy.',sections:[{title:'Translate common numbers',body:'GB (gigabytes) often describes RAM or storage—two very different things. Hz (hertz) describes refresh rate for screens or clock frequency in other contexts. W (watts) measures power, such as charging power. MP (megapixels) counts image pixels. nits describe display luminance.',bullets:['6 GB RAM and 128 GB storage are not interchangeable.','“Up to 120 Hz” is a capability; some screens vary their refresh rate.','Charging watts are a maximum supported rate, not a constant speed from 0–100%.','A benchmark score is a test result, not a complete measure of daily experience.']},{title:'Compare like with like',body:'Compare phones at similar prices and with the same test conditions. Check the exact model variant, region, RAM/storage configuration, network bands and update promise. Marketing names can hide differences between variants.'}],diagram:[['GB','Capacity','How much fits'],['Hz','Frequency','How often'],['W','Power','Rate of energy use'],['nits','Brightness','How bright']],example:{title:'Read this listing',body:'“8 GB + 256 GB, 120 Hz, 5,000 mAh, 67 W” usually means RAM + storage, advertised maximum refresh rate, battery charge capacity and supported charging power—in that order. Verify what the seller means by each number.'},myth:'“Bigger number always means better.” Numbers from different categories cannot be ranked against one another, and even the same number can be measured under different conditions.',takeaways:['Always learn the unit before judging the number.','Verify exact variant and testing conditions.','A single spec rarely predicts overall quality.'],quiz:[{q:'What do “8 GB + 256 GB” usually refer to in a phone listing?',options:['Battery + charger','RAM + storage','Camera + brightness','Refresh rate + power'],answer:1,why:'Listings commonly state RAM first and internal storage second, but verify the seller’s wording.'},{q:'Which unit describes charging power?',options:['Hz','MP','W','nits'],answer:2,why:'Watts measure power. Actual charging speed changes during a charge.'},{q:'What is the fairest way to compare two phone specs?',options:['Compare random numbers from ads','Compare exact variants and equivalent test conditions','Pick the biggest number in bold','Ignore software and price'],answer:1,why:'Variant, conditions, price and whole-device performance matter.'}]},
 {title:'The processor: CPU, GPU and NPU',level:'Beginner',mins:16,desc:'Learn the SoC, CPU cores, graphics and AI hardware without the jargon.',intro:'The SoC (system-on-a-chip) is a compact package that contains several kinds of computing hardware. The CPU handles general-purpose instructions, the GPU accelerates graphics and many parallel tasks, and an NPU or AI accelerator may speed up certain machine-learning workloads. A modem handles cellular communication.',analogy:'Think of a restaurant kitchen: the CPU is the head chef coordinating many jobs, the GPU is a large team chopping lots of vegetables in parallel, and an AI accelerator is a specialist station for a repeated kind of recipe.',sections:[{title:'Why chip names are not enough',body:'A newer or higher-tier chip often improves speed and efficiency, but real results depend on cooling, power limits, software, memory and the task. A short benchmark burst can look impressive while a long game may slow down after the phone heats up.',bullets:['CPU: app logic, operating-system tasks and general instructions.','GPU: 3D graphics, game rendering and parallel computation.','NPU/AI accelerator: selected on-device AI tasks, depending on app support.','ISP: image signal processor helps turn sensor data into a finished photo.']},{title:'Efficiency beats peak speed sometimes',body:'Phones have small batteries and limited space to remove heat. Efficient performance means completing work using less energy, not merely reaching a high peak score for a few seconds.'}],diagram:[['⚙️','CPU','General tasks'],['🎮','GPU','Graphics'],['✨','NPU','Some AI tasks'],['📸','ISP','Image processing']],example:{title:'Why two phones can feel different',body:'Two phones with similar chips may differ because one has better cooling, faster storage, lighter software or a higher-resolution display that is harder to drive.'},myth:'“More CPU cores automatically means faster.” Core design, speed, power limits, software and the job being run all affect performance.',takeaways:['A modern SoC combines several specialist components.','Peak benchmark speed is not the same as sustained speed.','Efficiency and cooling matter in small devices.'],quiz:[{q:'Which part mainly handles 3D game graphics?',options:['GPU','Battery','Storage controller only','SIM card'],answer:0,why:'The GPU specializes in graphics and parallel workloads.'},{q:'Why can a phone slow down during a long gaming session?',options:['The glass gets old instantly','Heat and power limits may reduce performance','RAM becomes permanent storage','The IMEI changes'],answer:1,why:'Thermal management may reduce chip speed to control temperature and energy use.'},{q:'What is the best comparison of two chipsets?',options:['Core count only','Name only','Real task performance, efficiency, cooling and software','The colour of the phone'],answer:2,why:'No single chip spec captures the user experience.'}]},
 {title:'RAM, storage and speed',level:'Beginner',mins:15,desc:'Understand multitasking, UFS, microSD and why free space matters.',intro:'RAM is fast short-term workspace. Storage is the long-term place for your apps, photos, downloads and operating system. More RAM can help a phone keep more apps ready, while faster storage can improve loading and file transfers. Neither creates a faster processor by itself.',analogy:'RAM is your desk; storage is your cupboard. A huge cupboard does not make the desk bigger. But if the desk is too small, you keep putting books away and fetching them again.',sections:[{title:'Capacity versus speed',body:'Capacity answers “how much fits?” Speed answers “how quickly can it be read or written?” A phone with 256 GB storage can still have storage that differs in speed from another phone with 256 GB. Available capacity also shrinks as system files and apps take space.',bullets:['RAM is usually not user-upgradeable in modern phones.','Storage almost always fills faster when photos, video and games grow.','A microSD slot (when present) may expand media storage, but usually not RAM.','Leaving some storage free can help updates and normal operations; exact behaviour depends on software.']},{title:'When do you need more?',body:'Light messaging and browsing need less than heavy gaming, large offline video libraries or frequent 4K recording. Choose enough capacity for your real use and expected years of ownership, not just a number that sounds impressive.'}],diagram:[['🧠','RAM','Apps in use'],['🗄️','Storage','Files kept'],['⚡','Speed','Transfer rate']],example:{title:'Choose wisely',body:'A student saving PDFs, photos and downloaded lessons may benefit from more storage. Someone switching between many large apps may benefit more from adequate RAM and well-optimised software.'},myth:'“256 GB storage means 256 GB RAM.” These are different types of memory with different jobs.',takeaways:['RAM = working space; storage = long-term capacity.','Storage capacity and storage speed are separate measurements.','Choose capacity around your use and ownership period.'],quiz:[{q:'Which is like the desk you work on?',options:['RAM','Storage','SIM','Screen glass'],answer:0,why:'RAM holds data apps are actively using.'},{q:'You have many videos but rarely play many apps at once. Which matters especially?',options:['Storage capacity','Number of rear camera rings','Refresh rate alone','Bluetooth name'],answer:0,why:'Videos consume long-term storage space.'},{q:'What does a microSD card usually expand?',options:['CPU cores','Internal working RAM','File storage','Screen brightness'],answer:2,why:'A microSD card adds storage for supported files/apps, depending on the phone.'}]},
 {title:'Displays: LCD, OLED, resolution and Hz',level:'Beginner',mins:16,desc:'Learn sharpness, colours, brightness, refresh rate and touch response.',intro:'A phone display is a grid of tiny picture elements called pixels. Resolution tells you how many pixels are available. Pixel density depends on resolution and screen size. LCD screens use a backlight; OLED pixels emit their own light and can switch off individually, which enables very deep blacks.',analogy:'Resolution is the number of tiles in a mosaic; screen size is how big the mosaic is. More tiles on a smaller mosaic usually means finer detail—but your eyesight and viewing distance also matter.',sections:[{title:'The display words',body:'Refresh rate (Hz) is how often the screen can update each second. A higher refresh rate can make scrolling and supported games feel smoother. Touch sampling rate is a separate measurement relating to how often touch input is checked. Peak brightness claims may refer to special conditions rather than full-screen sustained brightness.',bullets:['OLED: strong contrast and deep blacks; quality and longevity vary by panel and use.','LCD: uses a backlight; can still be bright and colour-accurate.','Resolution: pixels across × pixels down; not the only factor in perceived sharpness.','Outdoor readability also depends on brightness, reflections and automatic-brightness behaviour.']},{title:'Choose for the eyes and task',body:'Read outdoors? Prioritise sunlight readability and reflections. Watch movies? Contrast, colour tuning and speaker quality matter. Scroll or game? Smooth refresh and responsive touch may help, but app/game support is required.'}],diagram:[['▦','Resolution','Pixel count'],['🔄','Refresh rate','Updates per sec'],['☀️','Brightness','Outdoor visibility'],['⚫','Contrast','Dark vs light']],example:{title:'Practical comparison',body:'A well-calibrated 1080p OLED can look better than a poorly tuned higher-resolution screen. Compare side by side at the same brightness and content when possible.'},myth:'“120 Hz means every app shows 120 frames per second.” Refresh rate is screen capability; content, app, game and power settings determine what is actually shown.',takeaways:['Resolution and pixel density are related but not identical.','Refresh rate is different from touch sampling rate.','Brightness, reflections, calibration and efficiency also matter.'],quiz:[{q:'What does a 120 Hz display capability describe?',options:['Battery cycles','Potential screen refreshes per second','Number of camera megapixels','Storage speed'],answer:1,why:'Hz counts cycles per second; software and content can run at lower rates.'},{q:'Why can OLED show very deep black?',options:['Every OLED pixel can turn its light off','It always has more pixels','It has no battery','It uses a faster SIM'],answer:0,why:'OLED pixels emit their own light rather than relying on one backlight.'},{q:'Which measurement is closest to screen sharpness in context?',options:['Phone price alone','Pixel density along with viewing distance','Charger wattage','Speaker count'],answer:1,why:'Pixels per inch and normal viewing distance affect how detail is perceived.'}]}
 ]}
];
// Continue Phone Mastery: imaging, power, connectivity, privacy and buying.
/* The array is extended in a separate, straightforward block so each lesson remains easy to maintain. */
COURSES[0].lessons.push(
 {title:'Cameras: sensors, lenses and software',level:'Intermediate',mins:17,desc:'Why megapixels are only one part of a good photo.',intro:'A phone camera is a system: lens, sensor, stabilisation, image processor and software. The sensor collects light; the lens focuses it; image processing balances exposure, colour, noise and sharpness. The best result depends on lighting and what you photograph.',analogy:'Megapixels are like the number of dots in a painting. More dots can capture finer detail, but if the lighting is poor or the lens is blurry, extra dots cannot magically fix everything.',sections:[{title:'What shapes a photograph?',body:'Sensor size and pixel design affect how much light can be collected. Aperture describes how open the lens is. Optical image stabilisation moves lens or sensor hardware to reduce hand shake. HDR combines information from different exposures or frames to preserve bright and dark areas.',bullets:['Main camera: often the all-rounder.','Ultra-wide: fits more into the frame, sometimes with edge distortion.','Telephoto: provides optical magnification where fitted. Digital zoom mostly crops/enlarges data.','Night mode, portrait blur and HDR use substantial software processing.']},{title:'Video is its own challenge',body:'Look for supported resolution and frame rates, stabilisation, autofocus, microphone quality, heat limits and recording duration. A phone that records a brief high-resolution clip may behave differently during a long recording.'}],diagram:[['💡','Light','Scene illumination'],['🔎','Lens','Focuses light'],['▦','Sensor','Captures data'],['✨','ISP + AI','Builds image']],example:{title:'Photo choice',body:'For family portraits, skin tones, focus and reliable shutter timing may matter more than a headline megapixel count. For distant subjects, a genuine telephoto camera can help more than heavy digital zoom.'},myth:'“108 MP always beats 12 MP.” Sensor size, lens, pixel processing, stabilisation and lighting can outweigh the megapixel count.',takeaways:['A camera is optics + sensor + processing, not just MP.','Optical zoom differs from digital crop/zoom.','Judge real sample photos and video in conditions you use.'],quiz:[{q:'What does optical image stabilisation help reduce?',options:['Hand-shake blur','The phone’s storage capacity','SIM registration','Screen reflections'],answer:0,why:'OIS mechanically compensates for some movement, especially useful in low light and video.'},{q:'Which is true about megapixels?',options:['They fully determine quality','They count image pixels but not the whole quality story','They measure battery life','They mean optical zoom'],answer:1,why:'Lenses, sensor, light and processing contribute significantly.'},{q:'For distant objects, what is usually preferable when quality matters?',options:['A genuine telephoto camera, where available','Always the widest camera','Turning brightness to maximum','More SIM cards'],answer:0,why:'Optical telephoto can preserve detail better than cropping a wide-camera image.'}]},
 {title:'Battery life and charging',level:'Intermediate',mins:16,desc:'Understand mAh, watts, heat, battery health and realistic runtime.',intro:'Battery capacity is often advertised in mAh, but runtime depends on the entire phone’s energy use: display, radio signal, processor workload, apps, temperature and software. Charging power (watts) is a rate of energy transfer, not a guarantee of constant speed.',analogy:'Think of the battery as a water tank and power use as a tap. A large tank lasts longer only if the tap is not flowing much faster. Heat is like friction: it wastes energy and can age parts faster.',sections:[{title:'Charge smart, not anxiously',body:'Phones slow charging as they approach full to control heat and protect the battery. Fast charging generally requires compatible phone, charger, cable and protocol. The highest wattage printed on a charger is not necessarily what your phone will draw.',bullets:['Heat is a major enemy of long-term battery health.','Use reputable, compatible charging equipment.','Battery protection or adaptive charging features can reduce time spent at full charge on supported phones.','Poor signal, bright screen, hotspot use and gaming can increase power use.']},{title:'Read mAh carefully',body:'mAh describes electric charge capacity. Comparing batteries with different voltages or device designs is more complex, so real-world screen-on time and controlled tests are more useful than mAh alone.'}],diagram:[['🔋','Capacity','Charge stored'],['⚡','Power','Energy per time'],['🌡️','Heat','Efficiency + ageing'],['📱','Workload','How fast energy is used']],example:{title:'Why battery reports differ',body:'One reviewer streams video on Wi-Fi at medium brightness; another plays a 3D game outdoors on mobile data. Their results can differ greatly even on the same phone.'},myth:'“A 100 W charger forces 100 W into every phone.” The phone negotiates supported power, and charging rate usually changes throughout the session.',takeaways:['mAh is not a direct battery-life score.','Heat and workload strongly affect runtime and battery ageing.','Use compatible chargers and check actual phone support.'],quiz:[{q:'What most directly measures charging power?',options:['GB','W','MP','PPI'],answer:1,why:'Watts measure power. The device and charger decide the negotiated rate.'},{q:'Why might a phone drain faster outdoors?',options:['Sunlight makes storage disappear','High brightness and cellular conditions may use more power','Pixels become RAM','mAh turns into Hz'],answer:1,why:'Display brightness and radio activity can raise energy consumption.'},{q:'What is a sensible battery-health habit?',options:['Keep the phone hot while charging','Use compatible equipment and avoid excess heat','Always use a damaged cable','Ignore device charging guidance'],answer:1,why:'Heat and poor-quality charging equipment can create risks or accelerate wear.'}]},
 {title:'Mobile networks and wireless tech',level:'Intermediate',mins:18,desc:'4G/5G, Wi‑Fi, Bluetooth, GPS, NFC and signal myths.',intro:'Wireless technologies solve different problems. Cellular connects a phone to a carrier’s network across a wide area. Wi‑Fi connects locally to a router or hotspot. Bluetooth is for nearby devices. GPS/GNSS helps estimate location from satellite signals, and NFC works at very short range for supported interactions.',analogy:'Think of transport: cellular is the city-wide bus network, Wi‑Fi is the local shuttle, Bluetooth is passing a note across a desk, and NFC is tapping two cards close together.',sections:[{title:'5G is not one speed',body:'Real cellular performance depends on spectrum bands, signal strength, congestion, carrier deployment, the phone’s supported bands and plan. A “5G” icon does not guarantee high speed everywhere. Wi‑Fi speed similarly depends on router, band, interference and internet connection.',bullets:['Wi‑Fi connects devices locally; internet access depends on the connection behind it.','Bluetooth is designed for short-range accessories and low-power connections.','GPS/GNSS can struggle indoors or near tall buildings because signals are weak.','NFC is commonly used for tap-style interactions; it does not replace all payment/security checks.']},{title:'Read connectivity claims',body:'Before buying, confirm the exact model variant supports the cellular bands used by your carrier and region. Import variants can differ. Dual-SIM capability does not necessarily mean two 5G connections are active together.'}],diagram:[['📡','Cellular','Carrier network'],['📶','Wi‑Fi','Local router'],['🎧','Bluetooth','Nearby devices'],['🛰️','GNSS','Satellite signals'],['💳','NFC','Very short range']],example:{title:'Slow internet checklist',body:'If video buffers, test another app, compare Wi‑Fi with mobile data, move closer to the router, check signal/congestion and restart the connection before blaming the phone’s processor.'},myth:'“A 5G phone will always be faster than Wi‑Fi.” Speed depends on local network conditions, plan, router, interference and the server you reach.',takeaways:['Different wireless radios do different jobs.','Network generation alone does not guarantee speed.','Check regional bands and variant details before importing a phone.'],quiz:[{q:'Which technology typically connects wireless earbuds to a phone?',options:['Bluetooth','NFC only','GPS','UFS'],answer:0,why:'Bluetooth commonly handles nearby accessory connections.'},{q:'What does GPS/GNSS mainly help determine?',options:['Storage capacity','Location using satellite signals','RAM size','Screen refresh'],answer:1,why:'GNSS receivers estimate position from satellite signals; indoor reception can be poor.'},{q:'What is true about a 5G icon?',options:['It guarantees maximum speed','It proves the battery is full','It indicates a network connection mode, not guaranteed speed','It means Wi‑Fi is off forever'],answer:2,why:'Coverage, spectrum, signal, congestion, handset and plan influence actual performance.'}]},
 {title:'SIM, eSIM, IMEI and phone bands',level:'Intermediate',mins:13,desc:'Understand the identity and compatibility basics of a phone.',intro:'A SIM or eSIM profile helps a carrier authenticate a subscriber. The IMEI identifies cellular equipment, while a phone number identifies a reachable subscription/line in everyday use. They are not interchangeable. Keep these identifiers private and use official channels for support.',analogy:'A SIM is like your membership credential, an IMEI is more like the serial number of the device, and the phone number is the address people use to call you.',sections:[{title:'Physical SIM versus eSIM',body:'A physical SIM is a removable chip; an eSIM stores a carrier profile electronically. eSIM support depends on phone model, region and carrier. Some devices support combinations of physical SIMs and eSIM profiles but may limit how many lines work at once.',bullets:['IMEI may be needed for legitimate warranty, carrier or lost-device reports.','Do not post full IMEI, account details or one-time passwords publicly.','Network band support and carrier provisioning affect whether service works.','A phone can be unlocked yet still have limited band support in a region.']},{title:'Check before purchase',body:'Confirm the exact model number, carrier compatibility, SIM/eSIM options, regional bands and seller legitimacy. Imported or refurbished units may have different support, warranty or activation conditions.'}],diagram:[['🪪','SIM / eSIM','Subscriber profile'],['🔢','IMEI','Device identifier'],['☎️','Phone number','Line contact'],['📡','Bands','Radio compatibility']],example:{title:'India buying check',body:'Before buying an imported handset, confirm bands and VoLTE/5G support with your actual carrier, plus official warranty coverage in India.'},myth:'“An unlocked phone is guaranteed to work perfectly on every carrier.” Unlocking removes one restriction; hardware bands, carrier features and region rules still matter.',takeaways:['SIM, IMEI and phone number have different roles.','eSIM availability varies by model and carrier.','Keep device identifiers and OTPs private.'],quiz:[{q:'What does an IMEI identify?',options:['The phone’s cellular equipment','Your screen size','Your Wi‑Fi password','A photo album'],answer:0,why:'IMEI is an equipment identifier used by mobile networks and support systems.'},{q:'Does “unlocked” guarantee every network feature will work?',options:['Yes, everywhere','No; bands and carrier support still matter','Only when the battery is empty','Only with Bluetooth'],answer:1,why:'Unlocked status does not create unsupported radio bands or carrier features.'},{q:'What should you avoid sharing publicly?',options:['A general phone model name','Your full IMEI and one-time passwords','A review of a phone case','The official product page'],answer:1,why:'Identifiers and authentication codes can be abused; share only with trusted official support when needed.'}]},
 {title:'Operating systems, updates and privacy',level:'Advanced',mins:18,desc:'Android/iOS, permissions, security patches, backups and digital hygiene.',intro:'The operating system manages the phone’s hardware and provides services that apps use. Security updates fix known vulnerabilities; major version updates can add features and change compatibility. A phone’s support promise is part of its long-term value.',analogy:'The OS is the building manager, apps are tenants, permissions are keys, and security patches fix broken locks. A fancy building with broken locks is still a bad place to keep valuables.',sections:[{title:'Privacy is a set of choices',body:'Grant apps only permissions that make sense for their purpose. A torch app usually has no ordinary reason to access contacts. Review location, microphone, camera, photos and background activity settings. Avoid installing apps from untrusted sources.',bullets:['Install system and app updates from official mechanisms.','Use a strong screen lock and enable account recovery options.','Back up important files; a backup is only useful if it can be restored.','Be careful with links, unknown APKs, fake support calls and unexpected OTP requests.']},{title:'How to evaluate update support',body:'Look for a written commitment covering OS upgrades and security updates, then verify it for the exact model and region. Years of support may start from launch, not your purchase date. Update policies can change, so check the manufacturer’s current official policy.'}],diagram:[['🔐','Lock','Screen + account'],['🧩','Permissions','Limited access'],['🛡️','Patches','Fix weaknesses'],['☁️','Backup','Recover data']],example:{title:'Permission test',body:'A navigation app may reasonably request location while you navigate. A simple calculator asking for contacts and microphone access deserves scrutiny.'},myth:'“iPhones/Android phones are automatically safe without updates.” No platform removes all risk; updates, app choices, account security and user decisions all contribute.',takeaways:['Update support affects security and useful life.','Review permissions and use official app sources.','Backups should be checked and restorable.'],quiz:[{q:'Why are security patches important?',options:['They increase megapixels','They fix known security weaknesses','They change every phone into 5G','They add storage automatically'],answer:1,why:'Security patches address vulnerabilities in system components.'},{q:'A basic calculator asks for contacts. What is the sensible response?',options:['Grant everything without checking','Question whether the permission is needed','Send the permission request to strangers','Turn off the screen'],answer:1,why:'Permissions should match the app’s purpose and features.'},{q:'What should you check when comparing update promises?',options:['Only the marketing slogan','The written policy for exact model and region','The phone colour','The shop wallpaper'],answer:1,why:'Support commitments can vary by model, region and product launch timing.'}]},
 {title:'Build, water ratings and sensors',level:'Advanced',mins:14,desc:'IP ratings, glass claims, biometrics, accelerometers and thermal limits.',intro:'A phone’s physical design influences how long it survives daily use. IP ratings describe tested resistance to dust and water under specified conditions; they are not a promise that every accident is safe. Sensors such as accelerometers, gyroscopes and proximity sensors help the phone understand movement and its environment.',analogy:'An IP rating is like a raincoat test, not a promise you can wear the coat underwater forever, in soap, salt water and every possible condition.',sections:[{title:'Read durability claims precisely',body:'IP code digits describe specific dust and water-ingress protection levels under controlled test conditions. Resistance can weaken after drops, repairs and normal wear. Liquid damage may not be covered by warranty even when a rating is advertised.',bullets:['Accelerometer: senses acceleration and orientation changes.','Gyroscope: senses rotation; games and motion interfaces may use it.','Proximity sensor: helps detect when the phone is near your face during a call.','Ambient-light sensor: helps automatic screen brightness.','Fingerprint and face unlock systems vary in hardware and security design.']},{title:'Practical care',body:'Avoid intentional water tests unless the manufacturer explicitly permits that exact use. Dry the exterior before charging, follow the manufacturer’s liquid guidance and use a suitable case if drops are likely.'}],diagram:[['🛡️','IP rating','Tested ingress resistance'],['🧭','Motion','Acceleration + rotation'],['🌤️','Light','Brightness response'],['👆','Biometrics','Unlock identity check']],example:{title:'A careful interpretation',body:'An IP68 phone is not necessarily rated for hot water, soap, high-pressure jets or unlimited depth/time. Read the manufacturer’s exact conditions.'},myth:'“Water resistant means waterproof forever.” Resistance depends on test conditions, wear, damage and the liquid involved.',takeaways:['An IP rating has a defined scope and test condition.','Sensors detect different physical quantities.','Durability claims do not replace careful use.'],quiz:[{q:'What should an IP rating mean to a buyer?',options:['Unlimited waterproofing','A tested level of dust/water ingress protection under conditions','A battery grade','A camera zoom count'],answer:1,why:'The rating describes performance in standardised ingress tests, not every real-world accident.'},{q:'Which sensor helps detect rotation?',options:['Gyroscope','Storage chip','Speaker','SIM tray'],answer:0,why:'Gyroscopes measure angular rotation; accelerometers measure acceleration.'},{q:'What is sensible after accidental liquid exposure?',options:['Plug it in immediately','Follow the maker’s drying and liquid guidance; avoid charging while wet','Put it in an oven','Assume warranty will cover all liquid damage'],answer:1,why:'Charging while wet can be unsafe or damage the device; follow manufacturer guidance.'}]},
 {title:'Buying a phone and diagnosing problems',level:'Expert',mins:20,desc:'Compare phones by your needs, total value and a safe troubleshooting routine.',intro:'A good buying decision starts with your own workload and constraints. Set a budget, decide what must be good, and compare full-device performance rather than one headline spec. An expert can explain not just which phone to buy, but why that choice matches the person using it.',analogy:'Buying a phone is like choosing shoes. The most expensive pair is not automatically the best fit for your feet, daily route and budget.',sections:[{title:'A repeatable buying framework',body:'Prioritise software support, reliability, battery, display, camera, performance, storage, connectivity, repair options and price according to your use. Check the exact model and variant, recent independent tests, warranty terms and service availability.',bullets:['Student: storage for PDFs, readable screen, battery, updates and sturdy build.','Mobile gamer: sustained performance, heat management, touch response and controls.','Photo/video user: camera consistency, stabilisation, storage and audio.','Older/basic user: readable UI, long support, battery and simple service options.']},{title:'Troubleshoot without panic',body:'Start with the simplest reversible checks: note the symptom, check power/network/storage, restart, update apps/system, test in safe settings if available, and back up before a reset. Never share OTPs with “support” callers. Factory reset is a last resort after data backup and account checks.'},{title:'Look beyond the launch price',body:'Include charger/accessories, case, repairability, service availability, resale value and how many years the phone can remain securely useful. A discount is not a bargain if the exact device is unsupported or the seller is unreliable.'}],diagram:[['🎯','Your needs','Must-haves'],['🧾','Compare','Evidence + cost'],['🔒','Verify','Variant + warranty'],['✅','Decide','Best fit, not hype']],example:{title:'A sensible short list',body:'Give every candidate a score for support, battery, screen, camera, performance and price, weighting your top priorities more heavily. Then verify the top two in person or through reliable equivalent tests.'},myth:'“The newest launch is the smartest purchase.” Last year’s well-supported model can be better value; the best choice depends on price, needs, support and service.',takeaways:['Choose for your own use, not a spec race.','Check exact variant, update support, warranty and service.','Back up before major troubleshooting or factory reset.'],quiz:[{q:'What should you do before factory-resetting a phone?',options:['Back up important data and confirm account access','Post your PIN online','Remove all security updates','Ignore the problem description'],answer:0,why:'A reset can erase data and may require account credentials after setup.'},{q:'What is the best first troubleshooting step?',options:['Immediately pay a stranger to remote-access it','Observe the symptom and try basic reversible checks','Replace every part','Factory reset every time'],answer:1,why:'A structured, low-risk check can identify simple causes without losing data.'},{q:'Which phone is best?',options:['Always the highest-priced one','Always the one with the most megapixels','The one that best fits needs, budget, support and reliability','Any phone with a 5G logo'],answer:2,why:'The best purchase is a fit-for-purpose balance, not one headline number.'}]}
);
COURSES.push({id:'pc',name:'PC & Hardware Lab',short:'PC',emoji:'🖥️',color:'#20a890',banner:'banner-pc',level:'Beginner → Advanced',tagline:'Know every part before you build.',description:'Learn processors, memory, storage, graphics, motherboards, power, displays, compatibility, operating systems and troubleshooting.',duration:'6–8 hours',lessons:[
 {title:'What makes a computer?',level:'Beginner',mins:13,desc:'Meet the main components and how information flows through a PC.',intro:'A PC is a collection of specialised components. The CPU executes instructions, RAM holds active working data, storage keeps files, the motherboard connects parts, the power supply converts wall power for components, and the GPU creates graphics. Cooling helps components stay within safe operating limits.',analogy:'A PC is a workshop: the CPU is the team of workers, RAM is the workbench, storage is the warehouse, the motherboard is the roads, the PSU is the power station and cooling is the ventilation.',sections:[{title:'Desktop versus laptop',body:'Desktops commonly allow more upgrades, larger cooling and component replacement, but take more space. Laptops integrate parts for portability; many have limited or soldered upgrades. All-in-one PCs integrate the computer into the display and can be harder to repair.',bullets:['Input: keyboard, mouse, microphone and other devices.','Processing: CPU and often a GPU.','Memory: RAM and caches.','Storage: SSD or HDD.','Output: monitor, speakers, printer.']},{title:'A task through the system',body:'When you open a game, the OS reads program data from storage, brings active data into RAM, asks the CPU to run instructions, and sends graphics work to the GPU. The motherboard provides communication paths while the PSU and cooling system support the load.'}],diagram:[['🧠','CPU','General work'],['🧰','RAM','Active workspace'],['🗄️','SSD/HDD','Long-term files'],['🎮','GPU','Graphics'],['🔌','PSU + board','Power + links']],example:{title:'Example',body:'A PC can have a huge SSD and still feel slow if it has too little RAM, a weak CPU for the task, overheating or a cluttered operating system.'},myth:'“The tower is the computer.” The case is just the enclosure; capability comes from the parts inside, connected and configured properly.',takeaways:['A computer’s performance is a team effort.','Storage, memory and processing are different jobs.','Desktops and laptops trade upgradeability for different needs.'],quiz:[{q:'Which part is closest to the workbench analogy?',options:['RAM','Case fan only','Monitor stand','Power cable'],answer:0,why:'RAM holds active data the system needs quickly.'},{q:'What does an SSD primarily provide?',options:['Long-term file storage','CPU instructions by itself only','A bigger monitor','A Wi‑Fi signal from every PC'],answer:0,why:'An SSD stores the operating system, applications and files persistently.'},{q:'A powerful GPU will guarantee a fast PC for every task.',options:['Yes; GPU speed determines every task','No; the workload and other components matter','Only if the PC has huge storage','Only if the monitor is 4K'],answer:1,why:'Different tasks depend on different components and on cooling, memory, software and settings.'}]},
 {title:'CPU: cores, clock speed and cache',level:'Beginner',mins:17,desc:'Understand CPU specifications, single-core and multi-core work.',intro:'The central processing unit (CPU) executes instructions and coordinates much of a PC’s general work. Cores are processing units; threads represent execution contexts supported by the CPU. Clock speed indicates cycles per second, but one cycle does not do the same amount of work on every processor.',analogy:'A 4-worker team that solves problems quickly can beat a 12-worker team that works slowly or cannot divide the task well. More cores help only when software can use them effectively.',sections:[{title:'What the specs really say',body:'Modern CPUs often contain performance/efficiency core designs, caches and power-management systems. Cache is very fast, small memory close to the cores. Sustained performance depends on cooling, power limits and workload—not just the headline GHz value.',bullets:['Single-thread performance can matter for some apps and game logic.','Multi-core performance matters for well-parallelised workloads such as rendering.','Generational improvements and architecture matter as much as clock speed.','Desktop CPU compatibility depends on socket, motherboard support and BIOS.']},{title:'Choose for the workload',body:'Office work, browsing and study often need less CPU than video rendering, software compiling or complex simulations. A balanced system can feel better than one with an expensive CPU paired with too little RAM or slow storage.'}],diagram:[['🧩','Cores','Work in parallel'],['⏱️','Clock','Cycles per second'],['⚡','Cache','Fast nearby data'],['🌡️','Cooling','Sustained speed']],example:{title:'Real example',body:'Video export can use many cores, while some older apps depend more on strong single-core performance. Check benchmarks in the applications you actually use.'},myth:'“4.5 GHz is automatically faster than 4.0 GHz.” CPUs complete different amounts of work per cycle, and power/thermal limits matter.',takeaways:['Core count is only one part of CPU performance.','Clock speed is not a universal cross-model score.','Match the CPU to your software, budget and motherboard.'],quiz:[{q:'What is CPU cache designed for?',options:['Fast access to frequently used nearby data','Storing your whole photo library permanently','Cooling the case','Connecting to mobile networks'],answer:0,why:'Cache is small and very fast memory that reduces trips to slower memory.'},{q:'When are more CPU cores especially useful?',options:['When a workload can run in parallel','When selecting a wallpaper','For every app with no exceptions','Only when the monitor is bigger'],answer:0,why:'Multi-core gains depend on how well software splits work across cores.'},{q:'What else affects sustained CPU performance?',options:['Cooling and power limits','Keyboard colour only','SSD capacity only','The PC case logo'],answer:0,why:'Heat and power controls can limit how long a CPU sustains high clocks.'}]},
 {title:'RAM: capacity, channels and speed',level:'Beginner',mins:15,desc:'Choose capacity and understand DDR generations and dual-channel memory.',intro:'RAM is the system’s fast working memory. When applications need more memory than is available, the OS may move some data to storage, which is much slower and can cause stutter. RAM speed, timings, channel configuration and compatibility can also affect performance.',analogy:'Think of RAM as table space. If the table is too small, you repeatedly carry items to the cupboard. A bigger, well-organised table helps you keep more work open without extra trips.',sections:[{title:'How to choose RAM',body:'Capacity determines how much active data can fit; speed/bandwidth describes how quickly data can move under supported conditions. Dual-channel configurations can increase bandwidth on supported platforms. DDR4 and DDR5 are not interchangeable; motherboard and CPU support decide what works.',bullets:['Check how many memory slots the motherboard has.','Laptops may use replaceable SO-DIMMs or soldered memory.','Mixed kits can run at a lower common speed or behave unpredictably.','More RAM is most useful when the workload actually needs it.']},{title:'XMP/EXPO and rated speed',body:'Memory may boot at a safe default speed. XMP/EXPO profiles request tested higher settings, but support depends on CPU, motherboard, BIOS and the memory kit. Stability is more important than a screenshot of a high number.'}],diagram:[['📦','Capacity','How much fits'],['🚚','Bandwidth','How fast it moves'],['↔️','Channels','More data paths'],['✅','Compatibility','Board + CPU support']],example:{title:'Workload example',body:'For a basic study PC, enough RAM for the OS, browser tabs and apps matters more than chasing extreme memory speeds. Heavy editing or virtual machines can need much more.'},myth:'“RAM speed can compensate for having too little RAM.” Very fast memory cannot fix a capacity shortage that forces the system to page heavily.',takeaways:['Capacity and speed are different.','DDR generations and laptop/desktop modules are not universally interchangeable.','Check motherboard and CPU support before buying.'],quiz:[{q:'What happens if active work exceeds available RAM?',options:['The OS may use storage for paging, which is slower','The monitor becomes RAM','The CPU turns into an SSD','Nothing can ever change'],answer:0,why:'Paging/swap allows systems to keep working but storage is much slower than RAM.'},{q:'Can DDR4 memory fit every DDR5 motherboard?',options:['Yes, all DDR types fit','No, generations use different physical/electrical standards','Only if the case is large','Only when the monitor is 4K'],answer:1,why:'Memory generations are not interchangeable; follow motherboard specifications.'},{q:'What is the first priority when selecting RAM?',options:['Adequate capacity and compatibility','The brightest RGB lights','The package size','The highest number regardless of support'],answer:0,why:'Capacity and validated compatibility should come before cosmetic features.'}]},
 {title:'Storage: HDD, SATA SSD and NVMe',level:'Beginner',mins:16,desc:'Understand the drive types, M.2 connectors and real speed benefits.',intro:'Storage keeps data when the computer is off. HDDs use spinning magnetic disks and can offer low cost per gigabyte. SSDs use flash memory with no spinning disks. Many NVMe SSDs communicate over PCIe, often through an M.2 slot, but M.2 describes a form factor—not a guarantee of NVMe.',analogy:'An HDD is like a large archive room where a robot must find and fetch a folder. An SSD is like an electronic filing system. The archive may hold lots of boxes, but retrieving many small files takes longer.',sections:[{title:'Connectors and protocols',body:'SATA is an interface used by HDDs and SATA SSDs. NVMe is a protocol commonly used by SSDs over PCIe. M.2 is a physical card/slot form factor that can support SATA or PCIe/NVMe, depending on the device and slot. Read the motherboard manual.',bullets:['HDD: useful for large inexpensive archives and backups where speed is less critical.','SATA SSD: major everyday responsiveness improvement over HDD.','NVMe SSD: can offer very high throughput, especially for large transfers.','Real-world boot/app improvements depend on workload and other bottlenecks.']},{title:'Plan capacity and backups',body:'Keep free space for updates and temporary files; exact advice varies by OS and drive. Use a separate backup copy for important files. RAID/sync/cloud storage is not automatically a full independent backup if accidental deletion or account compromise can spread.'}],diagram:[['💽','HDD','Spinning disk'],['⚡','SATA SSD','Flash + SATA'],['🚀','NVMe SSD','Flash + PCIe'],['🛟','Backup','Separate copy']],example:{title:'Practical upgrade',body:'Replacing an old boot HDD with an SSD often makes an older PC feel dramatically more responsive, even without changing the CPU.'},myth:'“Every M.2 drive is NVMe and faster.” M.2 is the shape/connector family; the protocol and supported connection depend on the exact drive and motherboard slot.',takeaways:['Storage is permanent; RAM is working memory.','M.2 is not a speed standard by itself.','Backups should be separate and tested.'],quiz:[{q:'What is M.2?',options:['Always a CPU model','A physical form factor/connector family','A type of monitor brightness','An operating system'],answer:1,why:'M.2 may support different storage interfaces depending on the drive and motherboard.'},{q:'Which is usually the simplest storage upgrade on an older HDD-based PC?',options:['A compatible SSD for the OS/apps','A more colourful mouse','A larger monitor','A Wi‑Fi password change'],answer:0,why:'SSDs dramatically reduce access latency compared with mechanical HDDs.'},{q:'Why is a synced folder not always enough as a backup?',options:['Sync may also propagate deletion or unwanted changes','It makes CPU cores vanish','It turns SSDs into HDDs','It guarantees every file is private'],answer:0,why:'Independent, versioned copies help recover from deletion, corruption or account issues.'}]},
 {title:'GPU, VRAM and graphics',level:'Intermediate',mins:17,desc:'Learn integrated vs dedicated graphics, FPS, resolution and ray tracing.',intro:'The GPU processes graphics and other highly parallel tasks. Integrated graphics shares resources with the CPU/system, while a dedicated GPU usually has its own graphics processor and VRAM. Performance depends on GPU architecture, power limits, cooling, memory and the workload.',analogy:'If the CPU is an event manager, a GPU is a massive team of artists that can each paint a little part of the scene at once. VRAM is their nearby supply shelf for textures and graphics data.',sections:[{title:'Resolution, frame rate and settings',body:'FPS means frames per second. Higher resolution means more pixels to render; higher frame rates require repeated rendering more often. Effects such as ray tracing can be very demanding. Upscaling technologies may render internally at lower resolution and reconstruct a sharper output, with varying quality.',bullets:['VRAM stores textures, frame buffers and other graphics data.','A laptop GPU with a similar name may run at different power levels from a desktop version.','Benchmarks should match resolution, settings and game/app version.','For creative work, check app-specific acceleration and driver support.']},{title:'Avoid one-number buying',body:'A GPU choice depends on the monitor, games or apps, target settings, PC power supply, case space, cooling and budget. A fast GPU may be limited by CPU performance in some games or by VRAM in others.'}],diagram:[['🎨','GPU','Parallel graphics'],['🧱','VRAM','Textures + frames'],['🖥️','Resolution','Pixel workload'],['🎞️','FPS','Frames per second']],example:{title:'FPS trade-off',body:'At 4K, the GPU renders about four times as many pixels as 1080p per frame. A lower resolution or different settings can raise FPS, but visual quality changes.'},myth:'“A 12 GB GPU always beats an 8 GB GPU.” VRAM capacity is important, but architecture, speed, power, software and game requirements matter too.',takeaways:['GPU performance depends on task, power and cooling.','Resolution and settings strongly affect FPS.','VRAM capacity is only one part of graphics performance.'],quiz:[{q:'What does FPS stand for?',options:['Files per storage','Frames per second','Fan power system','Fast protocol signal'],answer:1,why:'FPS counts rendered/displayed frames per second.'},{q:'Which factor directly changes how many pixels a GPU must render?',options:['Resolution','Keyboard layout','SSD brand logo','Ethernet cable colour'],answer:0,why:'Higher resolution contains more pixels to render per frame.'},{q:'What should be checked when choosing a dedicated GPU?',options:['Only VRAM','Workload benchmarks, power, case fit, cooling and budget','Only the box art','Only the number of display outputs'],answer:1,why:'Compatibility and total workload performance are more useful than one headline spec.'}]},
 {title:'Motherboards, chipsets and expansion',level:'Intermediate',mins:16,desc:'Sockets, form factors, PCIe, M.2, ports and compatibility.',intro:'The motherboard provides connections between the CPU, RAM, drives, GPU, USB ports, networking and other devices. The CPU socket, chipset, BIOS support, memory type and board form factor determine what can be installed and which features are available.',analogy:'The motherboard is the road map and junction system of a town. A wide, modern road is useful only if your vehicles, bridges and destinations support it.',sections:[{title:'Know the compatibility checks',body:'CPU socket compatibility is necessary but may not be sufficient: the board also needs the right chipset/BIOS support. RAM generation, memory capacity, expansion slots, M.2/SATA sharing, rear I/O and Wi‑Fi/Bluetooth options vary by board.',bullets:['ATX, microATX and Mini-ITX are common size formats.','PCIe generations and lane counts affect expansion bandwidth; backward compatibility details depend on devices.','A board may share lanes or disable certain ports when specific slots are populated.','The board manual is the authoritative compatibility map.']},{title:'I/O for real life',body:'Count the ports and functions you actually need: USB-A/C, display outputs, wired Ethernet, audio, Wi‑Fi antenna connectors and internal headers. USB-C describes the connector shape; not every USB-C port supports identical speeds, video or charging.'}],diagram:[['🧠','CPU socket','Processor fit'],['🧬','RAM slots','Memory type'],['🎮','PCIe','GPU + cards'],['💾','M.2/SATA','Storage'],['🔌','I/O','External devices']],example:{title:'Before ordering parts',body:'Make a compatibility list: CPU ↔ motherboard/BIOS, RAM ↔ board, GPU ↔ case/PSU, cooler ↔ socket/case clearance, and drives ↔ supported slots.'},myth:'“If it physically plugs in, every feature will work.” BIOS support, electrical standards, lane sharing, firmware and device limits still matter.',takeaways:['Use the motherboard manual before buying.','Check CPU, RAM, storage and case compatibility together.','USB-C connector shape does not guarantee identical features.'],quiz:[{q:'What should you verify for a CPU and motherboard pairing?',options:['Socket, chipset/BIOS support and board specifications','Only matching brand','Only case colour','Only monitor refresh rate'],answer:0,why:'A physically compatible socket may still need supported firmware and platform features.'},{q:'Does every USB-C port support video output?',options:['Yes','No; supported functions vary by port and device','Only on desktops','Only with a hard drive'],answer:1,why:'USB-C is a connector type; data speed, display and charging support vary.'},{q:'Where should you check exact slot/port sharing rules?',options:['Motherboard manual/specifications','A random meme','Keyboard packaging','The wallpaper settings'],answer:0,why:'The board manual details specific M.2, SATA, PCIe and I/O limitations.'}]},
 {title:'Power supply and cooling',level:'Advanced',mins:16,desc:'Wattage, efficiency, connectors, airflow and thermal throttling.',intro:'The power supply unit (PSU) converts AC wall power into regulated DC power for a PC. It needs suitable capacity, quality, protections and the correct connectors. Cooling moves heat away from components using heatsinks, fans or liquid loops. Poor cooling can lead to noise, unstable behaviour or reduced performance.',analogy:'Wattage is not a quality rating any more than the size of a water pipe guarantees clean water. A good PSU is sized sensibly, built well and has appropriate protections.',sections:[{title:'Choosing a PSU',body:'Estimate system power from actual CPU/GPU specifications and independent system tests; allow sensible headroom for transient loads and future plans. Check reputable reviews, efficiency certification, warranty, protections and native connectors. Never mix modular PSU cables across models unless the manufacturer explicitly confirms compatibility.',bullets:['Efficiency ratings indicate conversion efficiency under defined loads, not the complete quality score.','Use the PSU maker’s guidance for GPU power connectors and cable routing.','Avoid overloading extension boards and damaged cables.','Never open a PSU; dangerous voltages can remain inside.']},{title:'Cooling and airflow',body:'Heat must travel from chip to heatsink and then to air or liquid moved away from the case. Balanced intake/exhaust, clean filters, correctly mounted coolers and unobstructed vents all matter. Liquid cooling is not automatically quieter, safer or higher-performing in every setup.'}],diagram:[['🔌','Wall AC','Incoming power'],['🔋','PSU','Regulated DC'],['⚙️','CPU/GPU','Heat created'],['🌬️','Cooling','Heat removed']],example:{title:'Upgrade example',body:'A new GPU may require more power, a different connector, extra case space and better airflow. Check all four before purchasing; PSU wattage alone is not enough.'},myth:'“A 1000 W PSU is automatically better than a quality 650 W PSU.” Too much capacity is not itself quality, and system needs vary.',takeaways:['Choose PSU quality and connectors, not wattage alone.','Cooling affects sustained speed and noise.','Never open a PSU or reuse unverified modular cables.'],quiz:[{q:'Why is PSU quality important?',options:['It supplies stable power with appropriate protection','It increases monitor pixels','It stores your files','It controls internet prices'],answer:0,why:'Power quality, protections, connectors and safe design matter for all components.'},{q:'What is thermal throttling?',options:['Performance reduced to manage temperature/power','A way to add RAM','A storage format','A kind of Wi‑Fi'],answer:0,why:'Components may lower clock speeds to remain within safe operating limits.'},{q:'Can modular PSU cables be mixed freely across units?',options:['Yes, always','No, pinouts can differ; verify manufacturer compatibility','Only if they are black','Only with an SSD'],answer:1,why:'Incorrect cabling can damage components and create safety hazards.'}]},
 {title:'Monitors, ports and peripherals',level:'Intermediate',mins:14,desc:'Resolution, refresh rate, panel type, response time, USB and docking.',intro:'A monitor transforms a computer’s output into visible pixels. Resolution affects detail, refresh rate describes how often a screen updates, response time describes pixel transition behaviour, and panel technology influences contrast, viewing angles and colour. The computer, cable and monitor must all support the desired mode.',analogy:'A monitor is a window onto the computer. More pixels add detail; higher refresh can make movement smoother; panel quality affects the colours and contrast you see through that window.',sections:[{title:'Choose a display for the job',body:'Office work benefits from readable text, ergonomics and enough workspace. Competitive gaming may value refresh rate and motion clarity. Photo work may need reliable colour performance and calibration. HDR labels vary widely in implementation and brightness.',bullets:['HDMI and DisplayPort versions/features affect maximum supported modes.','USB-C may carry display signal, data and power only when both ends support those features.','A laptop output can have limits based on the GPU and port wiring.','Ergonomics, eye comfort, glare and stand adjustment matter over long sessions.']},{title:'Peripherals and bottlenecks',body:'A webcam, microphone, headset, keyboard, mouse and external drive may improve the workflow more than a small upgrade to one internal spec. Check ports, USB power and the physical desk before buying.'}],diagram:[['🧮','Resolution','Pixel detail'],['🔄','Refresh','Motion updates'],['🎨','Panel','Colour + contrast'],['🔌','Port/cable','Supported mode']],example:{title:'Monitor example',body:'A 144 Hz monitor will not show 144 frames each second if the PC/game is producing only 55 FPS. Both output and display capabilities influence the experience.'},myth:'“A high-Hz monitor creates extra game frames by itself.” The source must render frames; the monitor can only refresh using available output.',takeaways:['Match resolution and refresh to the PC’s capabilities.','Confirm port, cable and device support.','Comfort and colour can matter more than headline refresh rate.'],quiz:[{q:'What is needed to actually experience high refresh in a game?',options:['A compatible display and a system producing enough frames','Only a fancy mouse','Only a large SSD','A printer cable'],answer:0,why:'The PC/game output and display mode both affect perceived motion.'},{q:'Does USB-C always carry charging, data and video?',options:['Yes, every port does','No, supported features vary','Only on phones','Only at 60 Hz'],answer:1,why:'Connector shape alone does not define every supported protocol or power mode.'},{q:'What is especially relevant to photo editing?',options:['Reliable colour performance and calibration','The most RGB lights','The number of case screws','The Wi‑Fi network name'],answer:0,why:'Colour accuracy can matter more than gaming-oriented refresh-rate claims.'}]},
 {title:'Build compatibility and first boot',level:'Advanced',mins:20,desc:'Plan the build, avoid common mismatches and understand BIOS/UEFI setup.',intro:'A successful PC build starts with a compatibility checklist before any parts are purchased. Confirm CPU support, memory type, cooler mounting, case dimensions, GPU length and power connectors, storage slots, front-panel headers and the PSU. After assembly, firmware setup and driver installation complete the system.',analogy:'Building a PC is like assembling furniture with electrical parts: checking the manual first saves you from discovering that one piece is for a different model halfway through.',sections:[{title:'Pre-build checklist',body:'Use manufacturer compatibility tools and manuals. Check that the case supports the motherboard size and GPU length; cooler height or radiator placement fits; the PSU has suitable wattage/connectors; and storage/PCIe slots will operate as expected.',bullets:['Work on a clean, well-lit surface and follow component instructions.','Handle parts by edges and follow anti-static precautions.','Never force a connector; shapes and keys indicate alignment.','If unsure about a power connector, stop and check the manual rather than guessing.']},{title:'First boot to desktop',body:'Firmware (BIOS/UEFI) initialises hardware and offers configuration settings. Confirm CPU and RAM are detected, check boot order, install an operating system from a trusted source, then install chipset/network/graphics drivers from official sources. Update firmware only with the manufacturer’s exact safe process.'},{title:'Stability matters',body:'Test memory and temperatures, check for errors and monitor behaviour under representative loads. Change one setting at a time and keep notes so you can undo unstable settings.'}],diagram:[['🧾','Compatibility','Before purchase'],['🧩','Assembly','Follow manuals'],['⚙️','BIOS/UEFI','Detect + configure'],['💿','OS + drivers','Finish setup'],['✅','Test','Stability + temps']],example:{title:'First boot checklist',body:'Fans and lights turning on do not prove everything is working. Check POST/diagnostic indicators, firmware detection, temperatures and OS stability.'},myth:'“Any warning can be solved by randomly changing BIOS settings.” Random changes can make booting harder; use exact manual guidance and one controlled change at a time.',takeaways:['Compatibility checking should happen before buying.','Use official OS and driver sources.','Test stability and temperatures before relying on the build.'],quiz:[{q:'When should you verify GPU length and cooler clearance?',options:['Before buying the case/parts','After throwing packaging away','Only if the PC is pink','Never'],answer:0,why:'Physical fit is a compatibility requirement, not a cosmetic detail.'},{q:'What is BIOS/UEFI for?',options:['Initialises hardware and provides firmware settings','Edits your phone photos','Adds more SSD capacity','Replaces the power supply'],answer:0,why:'Firmware helps initialise and configure hardware before the operating system runs.'},{q:'A connector does not fit easily. What is the safest action?',options:['Force it hard','Stop and verify orientation and manual','Cut off the plastic key','Use any cable that looks similar'],answer:1,why:'Forcing connectors can damage hardware; pinouts and keyed shapes matter.'}]},
 {title:'Operating systems and troubleshooting',level:'Expert',mins:20,desc:'Drivers, Task Manager, temperatures, backup strategy and a diagnostic method.',intro:'The operating system manages processes, memory, files, accounts, drivers and hardware access. Drivers let software communicate with specific hardware. Good troubleshooting begins by defining the symptom, reproducing it and changing one variable at a time.',analogy:'A good computer detective does not replace every part of a car because the radio stopped working. First collect clues, then test the simplest likely cause.',sections:[{title:'A calm diagnostic loop',body:'Record what changed, the exact error and when it happens. Check cables/power, free storage, task usage and temperatures. Restart and update from official channels. Use reliability logs or system tools where available. Back up important data before major repair steps.',bullets:['High CPU usage may come from an app, background task or malware; identify the process before acting.','Memory pressure can cause paging and stutter.','High temperatures and blocked vents can reduce sustained speed.','Repeated crashes may need RAM, storage, driver or hardware tests—not guesswork.']},{title:'Drivers and updates',body:'Prefer motherboard, laptop or component manufacturers’ official support pages. Avoid “driver booster” downloads and fake update pop-ups. Firmware/BIOS updates can be useful but carry risk if interrupted or performed with the wrong file; follow vendor instructions exactly.'},{title:'Backups and safe recovery',body:'Use independent copies of important files, including versioned or offline backups for valuable data. A restore point, sync service or RAID array may help with some failures but is not a substitute for a separate backup.'}],diagram:[['🔍','Observe','Exact symptom'],['🧪','Test','One variable'],['🛠️','Change','Low-risk fix'],['🛟','Back up','Before major repair'],['📋','Verify','Repeat the test']],example:{title:'Slow PC scenario',body:'Open Task Manager, sort by CPU/memory/disk use, note the process, check storage space and temperatures, then test a restart or app update. Don’t immediately reinstall Windows.'},myth:'“A cleaner app will always make a PC faster.” Many cleanup utilities add background processes or risk deleting useful files; use built-in tools and evidence first.',takeaways:['Define and reproduce the problem before changing things.','Use official drivers and updates.','Back up before resets, repairs and risky firmware work.'],quiz:[{q:'What makes troubleshooting more reliable?',options:['Change ten settings at once','Define the symptom and test one variable at a time','Download every driver site','Ignore error messages'],answer:1,why:'Controlled steps reveal cause and allow you to undo a bad change.'},{q:'Where should drivers usually come from?',options:['Official computer/component support sources','Random pop-up ads','Unknown file shares','A social media comment'],answer:0,why:'Official sources reduce the risk of malware and incorrect packages.'},{q:'What is a sound backup strategy?',options:['Only keeping one copy on the same drive','Independent copies with recovery tested','Only renaming the folder','Assuming sync can never delete files'],answer:1,why:'A separate tested copy helps recover from drive failure, accidental deletion and other incidents.'}]}
]});
COURSES.push({id:'cars',name:'Car Knowledge Garage',short:'Cars',emoji:'🚗',color:'#f1a345',banner:'banner-car',level:'Beginner → Advanced',tagline:'From first drive to informed buyer.',description:'Explore engines, gearboxes, handling, braking, safety, mileage, hybrids, EVs, Indian regulations and smart buying.',duration:'6–8 hours',lessons:[
 {title:'How a car works',level:'Beginner',mins:13,desc:'The major systems that turn energy into controlled movement.',intro:'A car is a coordinated machine designed to move people safely and comfortably. It needs a source of energy, a way to turn that energy into wheel movement, tyres to grip the road, steering to change direction and brakes to slow down. Electronics coordinate many systems too.',analogy:'A car is a team: the engine or motor provides effort, gearbox manages the relationship between motor speed and wheel speed, tyres grip the road, steering points the car, brakes slow it, and safety systems help protect occupants.',sections:[{title:'The main systems',body:'Powertrain means the components that create and deliver motion. Chassis systems include suspension, steering and brakes. The body provides structure and occupant space. Electrical/electronic systems operate lights, sensors, entertainment, driver assistance and engine/motor control.',bullets:['Engine or motor: converts fuel/electric energy into motion.','Transmission/reduction gear: adapts motor output to wheel needs.','Suspension: helps keep tyres in contact with uneven roads.','Tyres: are the only normal road-contact patches.','Safety systems: seat belts, airbags, ABS, ESC and structural design play different roles.']},{title:'Front-wheel, rear-wheel and all-wheel drive',body:'FWD sends drive to the front wheels and can package efficiently. RWD sends drive to the rear wheels and is common in some performance/load uses. AWD/4WD systems distribute drive to multiple wheels, but they do not repeal tyre grip or make braking distances vanish.'}],diagram:[['⚡ / ⛽','Energy','Battery or fuel'],['⚙️','Powertrain','Motor/engine + gears'],['🛞','Tyres','Grip the road'],['🧭','Steering','Choose direction'],['🛑','Brakes','Reduce speed']],example:{title:'Why tyres matter so much',body:'Even an expensive car with all-wheel drive cannot brake better than its available tyre grip and road conditions allow. Tyres influence acceleration, cornering, braking and comfort.'},myth:'“Four-wheel drive means the car can stop faster.” Drive system affects how force is delivered; stopping depends on tyres, brakes, speed and road surface.',takeaways:['A car depends on interacting mechanical and electronic systems.','Tyres connect every other system to the road.','More driven wheels do not guarantee shorter braking distances.'],quiz:[{q:'Which part makes normal road contact?',options:['Tyres','Roof lining','Infotainment screen','Number plate'],answer:0,why:'Tyres transmit steering, acceleration and braking forces to the road.'},{q:'What does a transmission/reduction gear help manage?',options:['Relationship between motor speed and wheel speed/torque','Air-conditioning colour','Seat-belt length only','Fuel quality by itself'],answer:0,why:'Gearing adapts source output to the vehicle’s speed and force requirements.'},{q:'Does AWD remove the need for good tyres?',options:['Yes','No, tyre grip remains essential','Only during monsoon','Only in an EV'],answer:1,why:'AWD can help deliver drive but cannot create unlimited grip or automatically shorten braking distances.'}]},
 {title:'Petrol and diesel engines',level:'Beginner',mins:17,desc:'Four-stroke cycle, cylinders, combustion and why engines need servicing.',intro:'An internal-combustion engine releases energy by burning fuel inside cylinders. In a common four-stroke engine, the piston moves through intake, compression, power and exhaust strokes. Petrol engines typically use spark plugs to ignite a fuel-air mixture; diesel engines ignite fuel from the high temperature of compressed air.',analogy:'Imagine a carefully timed bicycle pump with valves and a small controlled push on every cycle. The engine repeats a cycle rapidly in many cylinders, and turning forces move the crankshaft.',sections:[{title:'The four-stroke cycle',body:'Intake brings air (and fuel in many petrol systems) into the cylinder. Compression squeezes the contents. The power stroke releases energy from combustion to push the piston. Exhaust expels burnt gases. Many valves, sensors and control systems make this process efficient and reliable.',bullets:['Cylinder: the chamber where the piston moves.','Piston and connecting rod: translate pressure into crankshaft rotation.','Crankshaft: turns reciprocating piston motion into rotation.','Cooling and lubrication systems control heat and friction.']},{title:'Why service matters',body:'Oil lubricates moving parts, filters catch contaminants, coolant carries heat, and air/fuel systems need to function correctly. Follow the owner’s manual for service intervals, approved fluids and warning lights. Do not ignore overheating or oil-pressure warnings.'}],diagram:[['1','Intake','Air enters'],['2','Compression','Mixture squeezed'],['3','Power','Combustion pushes'],['4','Exhaust','Gases leave']],example:{title:'A small but important difference',body:'A petrol engine generally relies on spark ignition; a diesel relies on compression ignition. Both need correctly controlled fuel, air, lubrication, cooling and emissions systems.'},myth:'“Bigger engine always means more power and better performance.” Design, turbocharging, tune, vehicle mass, gearing and the specific power curve all matter.',takeaways:['The common four-stroke cycle has intake, compression, power and exhaust.','Petrol and diesel use different ignition methods.','Service schedules and warning lights protect reliability.'],quiz:[{q:'Which order describes a common four-stroke engine?',options:['Intake, compression, power, exhaust','Power, exhaust, intake, compression','Brake, steer, park, accelerate','Charge, coast, plug, reverse'],answer:0,why:'The four strokes repeat in intake, compression, power, exhaust order.'},{q:'How does a typical petrol engine ignite its mixture?',options:['Spark plug','Seat belt','Alternator only','Tyre valve'],answer:0,why:'Petrol engines commonly use spark ignition; diesel engines use compression ignition.'},{q:'What should you do with an oil-pressure warning?',options:['Ignore it until the next service','Follow the manual and stop safely as instructed','Accelerate harder','Wash the dashboard'],answer:1,why:'Oil-pressure problems can rapidly damage an engine; follow vehicle guidance and seek help.'}]},
 {title:'Torque, horsepower, RPM and gearing',level:'Intermediate',mins:16,desc:'Know pulling force, power and why gearboxes change how a car feels.',intro:'Torque is a turning effect. RPM counts rotations per minute. Power is the rate of doing work; horsepower is one unit used to express power. Engine torque and power vary with RPM, while gearing changes the torque and speed delivered at the wheels.',analogy:'Torque is like how hard you push a revolving door; power also considers how quickly you can keep doing work. Gearing is like choosing a bicycle gear for climbing versus fast riding.',sections:[{title:'Putting the terms together',body:'Wheel force depends on engine/motor torque multiplied by gear reduction and affected by losses and tyre radius. A low gear often multiplies torque at the wheels for launch or climbing, while a high gear lowers engine RPM at cruising speed. Electric motors can deliver strong torque at low speeds, but their power and gearing characteristics still matter.',bullets:['Torque: turning effect, often measured in Nm.','RPM: rotations per minute.','Power: work per unit time; commonly kW or bhp/PS.','Gear ratio: trades rotational speed for torque, with losses.']},{title:'Why peak figures are incomplete',body:'A vehicle’s real acceleration also depends on mass, traction, gear shifts, aerodynamics, throttle response, tyres and control software. Compare performance tests and the full torque/power curve rather than one peak number.'}],diagram:[['🔄','RPM','Rotations/min'],['↻','Torque','Turning effect'],['⚡','Power','Work per time'],['⚙️','Gearing','Wheel force / speed']],example:{title:'Bicycle analogy',body:'In a low bicycle gear it is easier to climb but you pedal more times; a high gear can cover more distance per pedal rotation but requires more force. A car gearbox similarly adapts output to conditions.'},myth:'“Torque and horsepower are the same thing.” They are related mathematically through rotational speed, but describe different aspects of output.',takeaways:['Torque is turning effect; power is how quickly work is done.','Gears trade speed for wheel torque.','Acceleration depends on the complete vehicle, not just engine numbers.'],quiz:[{q:'Which unit commonly describes torque?',options:['Nm','GB','nits','Hz only'],answer:0,why:'Newton-metres (Nm) are commonly used for torque.'},{q:'What is a low gear often useful for?',options:['Multiplying wheel torque for starting/climbing','Increasing phone RAM','Reducing tyre contact to zero','Making fuel unnecessary in every car'],answer:0,why:'A lower gear gives more torque multiplication, trading off wheel speed.'},{q:'What affects real acceleration besides peak power?',options:['Vehicle mass, gearing, grip and power delivery','Only paint colour','Only boot size','Only infotainment screen size'],answer:0,why:'Power is important but acceleration depends on tractive force, mass and the conditions.'}]},
 {title:'Gearboxes and drivetrains',level:'Intermediate',mins:17,desc:'Manual, AMT, torque-converter automatic, DCT, CVT and EV reduction gears.',intro:'A gearbox adapts power-source speed and torque to changing road conditions. Manual gearboxes are operated by the driver; automated systems control clutch and gear changes; CVTs vary the ratio continuously within their design; most EVs use a fixed reduction gear because electric motors operate across a wide speed range.',analogy:'A gearbox is like choosing bicycle gears. You need different ratios to start, climb, cruise efficiently and reach higher speeds without forcing the engine or motor to do the wrong job.',sections:[{title:'Common gearbox types',body:'Manuals use a driver-operated clutch and gear selection. AMTs automate much of a manual mechanism. Torque-converter automatics use fluid coupling and gearsets. DCTs use two clutches to prepare shifts. CVTs vary ratio without a conventional stepped gear sequence. Behaviour, efficiency and maintenance vary by design.',bullets:['Test low-speed crawling, reversing and hill starts during a safe test drive.','A gearbox that feels smooth to one person may feel different to another.','Service requirements and repair costs matter in long-term ownership.','EVs often use a single reduction ratio rather than a multi-speed gearbox.']},{title:'What to consider',body:'Look at reliability record, service schedule, traffic use, hills, fuel efficiency, response, resale and repair support for the exact make/model/year. No gearbox type is always best in every vehicle.'}],diagram:[['🦶','Manual','Driver shifts'],['🤖','AMT / DCT','Automated shifts'],['🔁','CVT','Variable ratio'],['⚡','EV reducer','Usually fixed ratio']],example:{title:'Indian city traffic',body:'An automatic can reduce clutch work in heavy stop-start traffic, but check low-speed smoothness, creep behaviour and repair history on the exact model—not just the transmission label.'},myth:'“Every automatic gearbox behaves the same.” Different designs can feel very different and carry different service/repair considerations.',takeaways:['Different gearbox designs solve the same ratio problem differently.','Test drive the actual vehicle in your normal conditions.','Maintenance history can matter as much as gearbox type.'],quiz:[{q:'What is the main purpose of a gearbox?',options:['Adapt torque and speed for driving conditions','Increase tyre size automatically','Replace the brakes','Control the seat-belt alarm only'],answer:0,why:'Gearing balances output speed and torque across different conditions.'},{q:'Which type commonly uses a continuously varying ratio?',options:['CVT','Manual only','Fixed differential alone','Seat adjuster'],answer:0,why:'A CVT varies ratio within its design rather than selecting conventional discrete steps.'},{q:'Which is the most useful transmission-buying approach?',options:['Judge only the name','Test the exact car and research service/reliability','Assume all automatics are identical','Ignore low-speed behaviour'],answer:1,why:'The experience and upkeep depend on the particular implementation and model.'}]},
 {title:'Tyres, suspension, steering and grip',level:'Intermediate',mins:16,desc:'Understand tyre size, road contact, ride, handling and wet-road limits.',intro:'Tyres are the only normal contact between the car and road. Suspension supports the car and helps maintain tyre contact on uneven surfaces. Steering directs the wheels; alignment sets their angles. Tyre compound, tread, inflation, temperature, water and road surface all affect grip.',analogy:'A car’s tyres are its shoes. Great shoes help, but wet tiles, worn soles and rushing still make slips more likely.',sections:[{title:'Read a tyre size',body:'A marking such as 195/65 R15 typically gives nominal section width in millimetres, aspect ratio, radial construction and rim diameter in inches. Load and speed ratings are additional details. Use the manufacturer-approved sizes and pressure recommendations shown on the car/owner manual.',bullets:['Underinflation can increase heat and wear; overinflation can reduce comfort and alter contact.','Tread depth and tyre age/condition matter, not just brand.','Wheel alignment and balancing solve different issues.','A wider tyre does not guarantee more grip in every condition.']},{title:'Grip has limits',body:'Braking, cornering and accelerating all demand grip from the tyre-road contact patch. Asking for a great deal of braking and turning at the same time can exceed the available grip. Leave safe distance, slow down for rain and follow legal speed limits.'}],diagram:[['🛞','Tyre','Contact patch'],['🌀','Suspension','Maintains contact'],['🧭','Steering','Wheel direction'],['🌧️','Conditions','Grip changes']],example:{title:'Wet road',body:'Rain can reduce grip and create standing water. Reduce speed, increase following distance and avoid sudden steering/braking rather than relying on a drive mode or large wheels.'},myth:'“Wider tyres always grip better.” Compound, pressure, tread, temperature, water, vehicle setup and road surface influence real grip.',takeaways:['Tyre condition and correct pressure are safety essentials.','Suspension and alignment affect control and tyre contact.','Grip is limited and changes with conditions.'],quiz:[{q:'What does “195” often represent in tyre marking 195/65 R15?',options:['Nominal section width in mm','Rim diameter in metres','Horsepower','The car’s fuel tank litres'],answer:0,why:'In that common format, the first number is nominal tyre width in millimetres.'},{q:'What is the safest reaction to wet roads?',options:['Drive faster because the car has ABS','Reduce speed and leave more distance','Brake and turn sharply','Ignore tyre condition'],answer:1,why:'Wet surfaces may reduce available grip and increase stopping distance.'},{q:'Does a wider tyre guarantee better grip?',options:['Yes, always','No; compound, tread, pressure and conditions matter','Only on every EV','Only if the tyre is new-looking'],answer:1,why:'Width alone does not define friction or water behaviour.'}]},
 {title:'Brakes and active/passive safety',level:'Intermediate',mins:18,desc:'ABS, ESC, airbags, seat belts, crumple zones and crash ratings.',intro:'Safety is layered. Seat belts and airbags help protect occupants in a crash; crumple zones manage crash energy; ABS helps prevent wheel lock under hard braking; electronic stability control (ESC) can help control a skid in certain situations. None can overcome physics or replace attentive driving.',analogy:'Safety systems are like a team of backups, not a superhero force field. They reduce risk or injury in specific situations, but the best crash is the one you prevent.',sections:[{title:'Passive and active safety',body:'Active safety aims to help avoid a crash: ABS, ESC, traction control, good tyres and driver-assistance systems. Passive safety helps manage injury when a crash happens: seat belts, airbags, head restraints and vehicle structure. Features and performance depend on exact variant and conditions.',bullets:['Seat belts should be worn by all occupants; airbags supplement belts rather than replace them.','ABS helps retain steering ability during hard braking, but may not shorten stopping distance on every surface.','ESC can help manage loss of directional control, but cannot defeat excessive speed or tyre limits.','ADAS features need correct use and may be limited by weather, markings and calibration.']},{title:'Crash ratings',body:'Look for a current, credible crash-test assessment that matches the exact model/variant/structure tested. Bharat NCAP publishes India-focused assessment information. Check test protocol, date, safety assist equipment and adult/child occupant results instead of assuming all cars from a brand have the same rating.'}],diagram:[['🛞','ABS','Helps avoid wheel lock'],['🧭','ESC','Helps control yaw/skid'],['🎗️','Seat belt','Restraint'],['🛡️','Airbag + structure','Manage crash energy']],example:{title:'Before a drive',body:'Seat belts for every person, correctly adjusted head restraints, properly maintained tyres/brakes and an attentive driver form the basics. Safety ratings add information—not permission to take more risks.'},myth:'“Airbags mean a seat belt is optional.” Airbags are designed to work with seat belts; always buckle up.',takeaways:['Active and passive safety perform different roles.','Belts, tyres, brakes and attentive driving matter in every vehicle.','Check ratings for the exact model and test protocol.'],quiz:[{q:'What does ABS primarily help with?',options:['Reducing chance of wheel lock during hard braking','Guaranteeing zero stopping distance','Replacing seat belts','Making tyres last forever'],answer:0,why:'ABS modulates braking to help prevent wheel lock and preserve steering control.'},{q:'Are airbags a replacement for seat belts?',options:['Yes','No, they supplement restraint systems','Only in the rear seat','Only at city speeds'],answer:1,why:'Seat belts are fundamental; airbags are designed as a supplementary system.'},{q:'What should you check in a crash rating?',options:['Exact model/test protocol and occupant protection results','Only the brand logo','Only the showroom music','Only the colour'],answer:0,why:'Different models, variants and protocols can yield different results.'}]},
 {title:'Mileage, fuel costs and ownership maths',level:'Intermediate',mins:16,desc:'Calculate running costs with km/L, kWh/100 km and annual distance.',intro:'A car’s cost is much more than its showroom price. Fuel or electricity, service, insurance, tyres, parking, tolls, repairs, loan costs and depreciation all contribute. Advertised mileage is measured under conditions that may differ from your traffic, route, load and driving style.',analogy:'A cheap printer can become expensive if its ink costs a fortune. Similarly, a car with a low purchase price can cost more over years if it consumes more energy or needs expensive repairs.',sections:[{title:'A simple cost formula',body:'For a liquid-fuel car, approximate fuel cost = distance ÷ actual km/L × price per litre. If you drive 12,000 km a year, get 15 km/L and petrol costs ₹100/L, fuel use is 800 L and approximate fuel cost is ₹80,000/year. This is an example; your actual mileage and price vary.',bullets:['Keep actual fuel-fill and odometer records for a realistic average.','A/C, traffic, short trips, tyre pressure, load and driving style can change mileage.','For EVs, energy use may be shown in kWh/100 km; home/public charging tariffs differ.','Compare total cost over your ownership period, not one metric.']},{title:'Depreciation and total cost',body:'Depreciation is the loss in resale value over time. Estimate total ownership cost as purchase/finance + running + maintenance/insurance/taxes/parking/repairs − expected resale, with assumptions written down. A vehicle with better efficiency may still cost more if its purchase and maintenance costs are higher.'}],diagram:[['🛣️','Distance','km/year'],['⛽','Efficiency','km per litre'],['₹','Energy price','₹/L or tariff'],['🧮','Cost','Distance ÷ efficiency × price']],example:{title:'Work it out',body:'12,000 km ÷ 15 km/L = 800 L. At ₹100/L, 800 × ₹100 = ₹80,000 per year for fuel. Add service, insurance, tyres and depreciation for a fair ownership comparison.'},myth:'“Best mileage on a brochure equals my daily mileage.” Real conditions differ; use consistent real-world figures and explain your assumptions.',takeaways:['Use real use-pattern data for fuel estimates.','Compare multi-year ownership costs, not only purchase price.','Write down assumptions to make comparisons fair.'],quiz:[{q:'At 12,000 km/year and 15 km/L, approximately how much fuel is needed?',options:['80 L','800 L','1,800 L','15,000 L'],answer:1,why:'12,000 ÷ 15 = 800 litres.'},{q:'At 800 L and ₹100/L, what is the fuel cost?',options:['₹8,000','₹80,000','₹1,00,000','₹800'],answer:1,why:'800 × ₹100 = ₹80,000.'},{q:'What is depreciation?',options:['Loss in resale value over time','Air pressure in tyres','Engine rotation speed','A seat adjustment feature'],answer:0,why:'Depreciation is the reduction in a vehicle’s value as it ages and is used.'}]},
 {title:'Petrol, diesel, CNG, hybrid and EV',level:'Advanced',mins:18,desc:'Compare powertrains by use case, cost, emissions and refuelling access.',intro:'Different powertrains store and use energy differently. Petrol and diesel cars burn fuel in an engine. CNG vehicles use compressed natural gas and require compatible equipment/refuelling. Hybrids combine an engine and an electric motor; plug-in hybrids can charge from the grid. Battery EVs use electric motors and rechargeable batteries.',analogy:'Imagine different lunch plans: one buys a meal as needed, one preps in bulk, one mixes two methods, and one carries a charged lunchbox. The best depends on availability, schedule and what fits your daily life.',sections:[{title:'Powertrain trade-offs',body:'Petrol can suit varied use and broad refuelling access. Diesel efficiency and torque can help some high-mileage applications but purchase, emissions-system and local-use considerations matter. CNG can reduce fuel cost where practical stations exist, but tank/package space and availability matter. Hybrids recapture some braking energy; EVs use electricity directly at the motor.',bullets:['EVs often have fewer routine powertrain service items, but tyres, brakes, cabin systems and battery health still matter.','Home charging access can strongly affect EV convenience and cost.','Public charging speed depends on vehicle, charger, battery condition and temperature.','Emissions depend on the whole energy/fuel supply chain and vehicle use—not only the tailpipe.']},{title:'Choose based on your route',body:'Estimate daily kilometres, highway/city mix, parking, charging/refuelling options, annual distance, local regulations, resale expectations and service support. A powertrain that is excellent for one home/route may be awkward for another.'}],diagram:[['⛽','Petrol / diesel','Liquid fuel'],['🟢','CNG','Compressed gas'],['🔁','Hybrid','Engine + motor'],['🔌','Battery EV','Grid → battery → motor']],example:{title:'Decision example',body:'A person with reliable home charging and mostly predictable daily trips should evaluate an EV differently from someone who regularly drives to areas without dependable charging.'},myth:'“EVs are always cheaper” or “EVs are always worse.” Cost and suitability depend on purchase price, energy prices, charging access, annual use, incentives, maintenance and resale.',takeaways:['Powertrains have different strengths and constraints.','Charging/refuelling access changes the ownership experience.','Compare your own use, energy prices and total cost.'],quiz:[{q:'What makes a hybrid different from a conventional ICE-only car?',options:['It combines an engine with electric motor/battery components','It never uses tyres','It requires no maintenance at all','It runs only on compressed air'],answer:0,why:'Hybrid systems combine an engine with electric-drive components; specific designs vary.'},{q:'What is especially important for many EV owners?',options:['Reliable charging access for their routine','A petrol can in the boot','The number of exhaust pipes','A manual clutch pedal'],answer:0,why:'Charging availability, location, time and tariff strongly affect convenience and economics.'},{q:'Can one powertrain be best for everyone?',options:['Yes, one technology fits all','No; route, usage, costs, infrastructure and needs differ','Only diesel is legal everywhere','Only EVs use brakes'],answer:1,why:'Suitability follows individual usage and constraints.'}]},
 {title:'EVs, batteries, charging and regenerative braking',level:'Advanced',mins:18,desc:'Learn kWh, charging power, battery health, range and regen.',intro:'A battery electric vehicle (BEV) stores electrical energy in a traction battery. Inverter/power electronics control the motor, which turns electrical energy into wheel motion. During regenerative braking, the motor can act as a generator and send some energy back to the battery; it cannot recover all the energy used to move the car.',analogy:'An EV battery is the energy tank, kWh tells you the tank’s usable energy amount, kW tells you the rate of power, and regeneration is like recovering some energy when slowing—not creating free energy from nothing.',sections:[{title:'kWh versus kW',body:'kWh (kilowatt-hour) measures energy; kW (kilowatt) measures power. A 60 kWh usable battery stores an amount of energy, while an 80 kW charge rate describes a momentary power rate. Charging curves change with state of charge, battery temperature, charger capability and vehicle limits.',bullets:['Range depends on usable battery energy and real-world efficiency (kWh/100 km).','Cold/hot weather, speed, hills, load, tyres and cabin heating/cooling can change range.','Regenerative braking is limited by battery charge state, temperature, grip and vehicle settings.','Fast charging often slows as the battery gets fuller to protect it.']},{title:'Battery life basics',body:'Battery degradation varies by chemistry, temperature, use and charging pattern. Follow the manufacturer’s guidance; avoid assumptions based on one viral rule. Check the warranty terms and battery-health evidence available for the exact vehicle when buying used.'}],diagram:[['🔋','Battery','Energy in kWh'],['⚡','Inverter','Controls power'],['🌀','Motor','Electric → motion'],['♻️','Regeneration','Some energy recovered']],example:{title:'Estimate EV energy',body:'If a vehicle averages 15 kWh/100 km, then 1,000 km uses about 150 kWh from the measured energy basis. Real charging-bill energy can be higher because of charging losses and conditions.'},myth:'“Regenerative braking means you never need friction brakes or charging.” Regen recovers only part of kinetic energy; regular charging and conventional brakes remain necessary.',takeaways:['kW is power; kWh is energy.','EV range varies with use and conditions.','Regeneration recovers some, not all, energy.'],quiz:[{q:'What does kWh measure?',options:['Energy','Power at a moment','Tyre width','Engine RPM'],answer:0,why:'A kilowatt-hour is a unit of energy.'},{q:'Why can DC fast charging slow near a high state of charge?',options:['Charging management protects the battery and follows its charge curve','The car forgets the destination','Tyres absorb electricity','The display has too many pixels'],answer:0,why:'Battery limits and control strategy usually reduce power at higher charge levels.'},{q:'What is regenerative braking?',options:['Recovering some kinetic energy into electrical energy','Creating unlimited energy','A feature that removes all brakes','A tyre pressure sensor'],answer:0,why:'The motor can generate electricity while slowing, but recovery is limited by physics and system conditions.'}]},
 {title:'Car sizes, body styles and practical specs',level:'Intermediate',mins:15,desc:'Hatchback, sedan, SUV, wheelbase, ground clearance and boot space.',intro:'Body style affects packaging and practicality, but labels such as “SUV” do not fully describe a vehicle. Wheelbase is the distance between the front and rear axle centres; ground clearance is the distance from a defined low point to the ground under specified conditions. Boot capacity methods can differ across manufacturers.',analogy:'Shopping for a car is like choosing a backpack: the outside look matters, but the compartments, access, weight and fit with your everyday items decide whether it works.',sections:[{title:'Specs you can feel',body:'Wheelbase can influence cabin packaging and ride behaviour alongside suspension and chassis tuning. Ground clearance may help over some uneven surfaces, but approach/departure angles, wheelbase, tyres, load and driving skill also affect obstacle clearance. A larger boot headline does not always mean it fits your suitcases easily.',bullets:['Hatchback: often compact with a rear hatch and flexible cargo opening.','Sedan: separate boot compartment and a distinct three-box shape.','SUV/crossover: typically higher stance; proportions and capability vary widely.','MPV: often prioritises flexible passenger seating and family practicality.']},{title:'Check fit in the real world',body:'Bring your actual use cases to a showroom: child seat, luggage, parking space, family entry/exit and bad-road route. Inspect rear seat comfort, visibility, spare-wheel packaging, turning circle and actual loading height.'}],diagram:[['📏','Wheelbase','Axle to axle'],['↕️','Clearance','Low point to road'],['🧳','Boot','Cargo usability'],['↪️','Turning circle','Space to turn']],example:{title:'Family test',body:'Do not choose only by the biggest boot litres. Open the hatch, load your regular bags, fold seats and check the opening shape and loading lip.'},myth:'“Higher ground clearance automatically means better off-road ability.” Tyres, gearing, protection, angles, traction systems and driver skill also matter.',takeaways:['Body-style labels hide real design differences.','Inspect practical fit, not just catalogue litres.','Clearance is one part of a vehicle’s capability.'],quiz:[{q:'What is wheelbase?',options:['Distance between front and rear axle centres','Height of the roof','Fuel tank capacity','Distance travelled per litre'],answer:0,why:'Wheelbase is the longitudinal distance between the axle centres.'},{q:'What is the best way to verify boot practicality?',options:['Load your actual luggage and test the opening','Choose only the largest advertised number','Measure the infotainment screen','Check the horn sound'],answer:0,why:'Shape, opening height and usable layout determine if cargo fits.'},{q:'Does high ground clearance alone make a car good off-road?',options:['Yes, always','No; many other geometry, traction and durability factors matter','Only on motorways','Only in electric cars'],answer:1,why:'Clearance helps in certain situations but is not a complete off-road measure.'}]},
 {title:'Buying new or used in India',level:'Expert',mins:22,desc:'PDI, documents, test drives, used-car inspection and total ownership cost.',intro:'The best car purchase is safe, suitable and affordable to own—not simply the one with the longest feature list. Verify the exact variant, safety equipment, warranty, service costs, documents, ownership conditions and local usability. Use an independent inspection for a used car, especially when you lack mechanical experience.',analogy:'Treat a car purchase like a mini investigation, not an impulse purchase. A shiny bonnet is a good haircut for a car; it does not prove the engine, documents or accident history are healthy.',sections:[{title:'Before paying',body:'Compare on-road price and itemised charges, insurance, warranty, service intervals, waiting periods, features on the exact variant and resale assumptions. Test-drive on realistic routes, including low-speed traffic, a safe uneven stretch and parking manoeuvres.',bullets:['Verify Registration Certificate (RC), chassis/engine identifiers where applicable, insurance, PUC and service records.','Check for outstanding finance/hypothecation, ownership transfer steps and applicable state requirements through official channels.','For used cars, have a trusted independent mechanic inspect structure, engine/transmission, tyres, brakes, electronics and accident repairs.','Do not hand over full payment based only on screenshots or verbal promises.']},{title:'Pre-delivery inspection (new car)',body:'Inspect body panels and glass in daylight, tyres, lights, dashboard warnings, keys, spare/tool kit, accessories, odometer and documents before accepting the delivery. Confirm manufacturing details, variant and promised accessories; record any issue before signing acceptance.'},{title:'Safety and variant traps',body:'A model name can include variants with different airbags, ESC, ADAS, tyre sizes and crash-test applicability. Verify current official specifications and credible safety-test details for the exact version. Avoid assuming a brand-level rating applies to every car.'}],diagram:[['🎯','Needs + budget','Set priorities'],['🧾','Documents','Verify facts'],['🚘','Test drive','Use real route'],['🔍','Inspection','Independent check'],['✅','Deal','Read before signing']],example:{title:'A simple cost comparison',body:'Compare on-road price + financing + fuel/electricity + service + insurance + tyres + parking + expected repairs − resale value over the same ownership period.'},myth:'“A used car is safe to buy if it looks clean and the seller says ‘single owner’.” Appearance and verbal claims do not replace document checks, history and independent inspection.',takeaways:['Compare total ownership cost, not just showroom price.','Verify documents and exact variant with official/qualified help.','Get independent inspection for a used car when possible.'],quiz:[{q:'What should be checked on a used car?',options:['Documents, service/history and independent mechanical inspection','Only paint shine','Only seat covers','Only the music system'],answer:0,why:'Documentation and mechanical/structural condition are central to a safe purchase.'},{q:'Why must you verify the exact variant’s safety equipment?',options:['Equipment can differ across trims and model years','All variants are always identical','Colour sets the airbag count','The registration number changes the crash structure'],answer:0,why:'Features and test applicability can differ by variant/year/structure.'},{q:'Which estimate is most useful for budgeting?',options:['Only ex-showroom price','Total ownership costs over the planned period','Only monthly fuel in the best case','The most expensive accessory list'],answer:1,why:'Running, service, insurance, finance, depreciation and resale all affect total cost.'}]}
]});

